1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,427
The brush uncovers a small figure in bronze, slender and standing.

2
00:00:05,427 --> 00:00:13,025
It was cast by the lost-wax method: model it in wax, cover it in clay, melt the wax out, pour the metal in.

3
00:00:17,567 --> 00:00:21,891
Four and a half thousand years ago, somebody in this world knew how to do that.

4
00:00:21,891 --> 00:00:25,867
And then, within a few centuries, the cities they lived in emptied.

5
00:00:28,833 --> 00:00:29,844
One source again.

6
00:00:29,844 --> 00:00:31,640
No text tells us what happened.

7
00:00:31,640 --> 00:00:36,467
The evidence for the end of the Harappan cities is the ground itself: what was built,

8
00:00:36,467 --> 00:00:40,920
what stopped being built, and what the rivers and the climate were doing.

9
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:46,557
First, the map of this world — and it was a big one.

10
00:00:46,557 --> 00:00:48,158
Harappa, on the Ravi.

11
00:00:48,158 --> 00:00:50,196
Mohenjo-daro, on the Indus.

12
00:00:50,196 --> 00:00:52,597
Dholavira, in the Rann of Kutch.

13
00:00:52,597 --> 00:00:55,444
Lothal, near the Gulf of Khambhat.

14
00:00:59,367 --> 00:01:01,263
Kalibangan, in Rajasthan.

15
00:01:01,263 --> 00:01:05,347
Rakhigarhi in Haryana, one of the largest sites of all.

16
00:01:05,347 --> 00:01:06,076
Banawali.

17
00:01:06,076 --> 00:01:06,951
Chanhudaro.

18
00:01:06,951 --> 00:01:12,867
Settlements spread across a region larger than many empires that came later.

19
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:16,382
Dholavira deserves its own moment.

20
00:01:16,382 --> 00:01:18,774
It is laid out in three parts rather than two,

21
00:01:18,774 --> 00:01:22,948
and it is built where there is almost no fresh water — so its people cut enormous

22
00:01:22,948 --> 00:01:27,372
reservoirs into the rock and channelled every drop of seasonal runoff into them.

23
00:01:32,067 --> 00:01:37,853
They also left a signboard: large signs, set in a row, mounted where they could be seen.

24
00:01:37,853 --> 00:01:39,388
We cannot read it.

25
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,945
Lothal has a great brick basin linked to a channel, widely read as a dockyard.

26
00:01:46,945 --> 00:01:52,567
Kalibangan has rows of pits with ash and burnt material, usually called fire altars.

27
00:01:57,833 --> 00:02:03,998
Rakhigarhi, in Haryana, is one of the largest Harappan sites known, and is still being excavated.

28
00:02:03,998 --> 00:02:07,380
Each site adds something the others do not have.

29
00:02:09,367 --> 00:02:10,346
Now the end.

30
00:02:10,346 --> 00:02:13,283
From about 1900 BCE the cities change.

31
00:02:13,283 --> 00:02:15,543
Drains stop being maintained.

32
00:02:15,543 --> 00:02:17,727
Large houses are divided up.

33
00:02:17,727 --> 00:02:21,268
The standard brick gives way to reused rubble.

34
00:02:21,268 --> 00:02:23,377
Writing on seals fades out.

35
00:02:23,377 --> 00:02:25,710
Long-distance trade thins.

36
00:02:27,467 --> 00:02:30,921
But — and this matters — the people do not vanish.

37
00:02:30,921 --> 00:02:34,986
Settlement shifts and spreads, and village life carries on,

38
00:02:34,986 --> 00:02:40,502
in what archaeologists call the Late Harappan phase, down to about 1300 BCE.

39
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:46,298
Why?

40
00:02:46,298 --> 00:02:49,751
This is one of the real open questions in Indian history.

41
00:02:49,751 --> 00:02:53,859
One group of explanations is environmental: rivers shifted or dried,

42
00:02:53,859 --> 00:02:59,395
the monsoon weakened, and the farming and trade that supported dense cities no longer could.

43
00:02:59,395 --> 00:03:07,078
Another is economic and social: trade with Mesopotamia declined, and the arrangements that held a city together came apart.

44
00:03:13,333 --> 00:03:17,405
You will also meet an older claim that invaders destroyed the cities.

45
00:03:17,405 --> 00:03:22,524
Most archaeologists now reject it, because the evidence for a violent end is not there.

46
00:03:22,524 --> 00:03:27,885
The likeliest answer is not one cause but several, acting together over two centuries.

47
00:03:32,367 --> 00:03:34,978
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

48
00:03:34,978 --> 00:03:37,844
These cities were built where the water was.

49
00:03:37,844 --> 00:03:39,673
Water came, water went.

50
00:03:44,167 --> 00:03:44,887
Dig Quiz.

51
00:03:44,887 --> 00:03:46,689
Pause and name the site.

52
00:03:46,689 --> 00:03:53,104
First: a city in three parts, with great reservoirs cut into rock, in the Rann of Kutch.

53
00:03:53,104 --> 00:03:54,197
Dholavira.

54
00:03:58,033 --> 00:04:03,720
Second: a brick basin linked by a channel to a river, near the Gulf of Khambhat.

55
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:04,281
Lothal.

56
00:04:04,281 --> 00:04:10,108
Third: one of the largest Harappan sites known, in Haryana, still being excavated.

57
00:04:10,108 --> 00:04:11,251
Rakhigarhi.

58
00:04:13,233 --> 00:04:18,351
In Mains, a standing question asks you to assess the theories for Harappan decline.

59
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:26,664
The marks are in the assessment: give the environmental and economic cases their weight,

60
00:04:26,664 --> 00:04:34,029
say plainly why the invasion theory is no longer accepted, and conclude that the evidence points to several causes rather than one.

61
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:44,999
A bronze figurine takes its place, and the first room of the gallery is full: six cases, from a hand axe to a city.

62
00:04:48,700 --> 00:04:52,504
Next gallery: the Vedas, and the world that comes next.

63
00:04:52,504 --> 00:04:56,677
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

